  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Unfinished Histories is showing clear early use: it has been downloaded 4,353 times and has already been cited 6 times. The record also shows institutional support from KU Leuven and open availability through a hybrid-access route, which likely helps its reach.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Published by Leuven University Press in 2022, Unfinished Histories examines the afterlives of Belgian colonialism across Belgium and the Democratic Republic of the Congo, Rwanda, and Burundi. Its interdisciplinary framing, spanning literature, politics, the arts, media, cinema, museal practice, architecture, language policy, justice, and ethics, aligns with the way it is being taken up in the scholarly record.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest impact signal is usage. With 4,353 downloads recorded through OAPEN, the book has already attracted substantial reading attention, suggesting that it is being consulted beyond a narrow specialist audience. That pattern is consistent with a work that speaks to several overlapping fields, including colonial history, politics, sociology, the arts, and public memory.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Citation evidence is still modest but meaningful for a recent scholarly book: OpenAlex records 6 citations, indicating that the book has begun to enter academic discussion. The citing works are recent and appear in journals such as The Journal of Imperial &amp; Commonwealth History, Archives and Records, Frontiers in Sociology, International Journal of Sport Policy and Politics, and Soccer and Society. Together, they show that the book is being used not only in colonial history but also in research on archives, decolonisation, and sport-related questions shaped by imperial legacies.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The pattern of citation also suggests cross-disciplinary resonance rather than concentration in a single subfield. The citing authors and institutions span several countries, with repeated use in the United Kingdom and Belgium and additional appearances in Rwanda, Mexico, and the United States. That breadth fits the book\u2019s comparative scope and its focus on how colonial legacies persist in different institutional and cultural settings.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Some forms of impact are not evidenced in the current record, so they should not be inferred. There are no event records in the supplied data, and the public-discourse archive is unavailable. Even so, the available signals already show a book that is being read and cited in ways that extend its reach across humanities and social-science conversations about colonial memory and decolonisation.</p>",
